  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.5 × loss over x, z in [−2, 2]. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first. Swings grow when learning rate × 2.4 > 2, so for rates above about 0.83.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with SD 1.5·√((64−B)/(63·B)) per direction. This is 1.5 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64, the whole simulated data set.",
    "Orange arrow: the next mini-batch step, the way the ball actually moves. Grey arrow: learning rate × the true downhill slope. Both are drawn at their true length (scale 1 bowl unit per unit).",
    "A run stops at 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 8 (diverged). Off the bowl, the ball and path are drawn at its edge, but the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed and the run number, so Reset replays the same sequence. 'New run' uses fresh noise and keeps the last path as the ghost. The ball takes 5 steps per second at speed 1."
  ]
}